I'm amazed that nethack is such a portable game, yet the
save files and bonefiles are incompatible. There is a simple solution
to this: and that is a portable bone/save file.

Actually, portable save file is unimportant IMO, what we want is
a good set of bonefiles that we can share all together.

I decided to begin whacking at this problem. To reduce wasted or
duplicated effort, I'll tell you what I've discovered in the past hour:

==== Getting upto the Bone File, starting with death ====

1. The important files are end.c, bones.c, and save.c.

2. When you die, the function "done(how)" is called in src/end.c.
The possible values for "how" are found in include/hack.h.
Here they are:

#define DIED         0
#define CHOKING      1
#define POISONING    2
#define STARVING     3
#define DROWNING     4
#define BURNING      5
#define DISSOLVED    6
#define CRUSHING     7
#define STONING      8
#define TURNED_SLIME     9
#define GENOCIDED   10
#define PANICKED    11
#define TRICKED     12
#define QUIT        13
#define ESCAPED     14
#define ASCENDED    15

(Quick note to patch makers: Everything smaller than "genocided"
(that is 9 and below) is saved by amulet of life saving. If you somehow
make a new kind of death, add it after GENOCIDED if life saving
doesn't save you, and before if it can)


3. Most of the done() is unimportant to bone files, so skip to line
726 for bones. You can see that done() calls "savebones()", which
is found in src/bones.c line 193.

4. The last "setup" before making the actual bonefile
is on line 318.

    fd = create_bonesfile(&u.uz, &bonesid, whynot);

This basically just makes the file in the form of bonT0.0 or
whatever it is, and then returns the file descriptor.

==== A quick note====

If you are wondering what bwrite and bclose and all
that are, it is basically a wrapper around fwrite, but portable,
and buffering is handled by nethack as opposed to the C library
or whatnot.

=== The actual bonefile creation ===

The big #ifdef at line 332 just checks if there is enough room on the
floppy disk. Basically, looks like we are going to have to duplicate
efforts here :-/ Whatever is done filewise after the #ifdef, should be
repeated here... i think... so that the floppy's space can be accuratly
judged.

Basically, ignore it for now. It just gets in the way of figuring out
what c and bonsid are.

Overall, the reason why bone files are unportable is because
they are written in the raw memory form. I figure other problems
are endianness and

Anyway, the big part is here, line 363:

    store_version(fd);
    bwrite(fd, (genericptr_t) &c, sizeof c);
    bwrite(fd, (genericptr_t) bonesid, (unsigned) c);   /* DD.nnn */
    savefruitchn(fd, WRITE_SAVE | FREE_SAVE);
    update_mlstmv();    /* update monsters for eventual restoration */
    savelev(fd, ledger_no(&u.uz), WRITE_SAVE | FREE_SAVE);
    bclose(fd);
    commit_bonesfile(&u.uz);
    compress_bonesfile();

store_version is found in version.c, and is dependant on the
structure "struct version_info". So first thing in all
files is a description of the current version (including a
couple of compile-time flags). This definitly neds
to be changed for portability (size of struct version_info
is different from compile to compile)

bwrite and bclose is save.c. Simply wrappers to writing
functions. Here, it basically writes the length of whatever
comes after "bon" in the filename, and then that string (including
the \0, nul terminator)

*savefruitchn is in save.c. Seems to save the names of
your favorite fruit :-/ (wow, level of detail is still amazing)

*savelev is in save.c. This is the core of bonefiles. It simply
writes the raw level dump into the file. Definitly needs to be
changed for portability.

commit_bonesfile is in files.c. It basically changes the
name of the bonesfile, and saved it in the appropriate
place.

